#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128773 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab643519-92a4-5ccf-b826-fb2a68b36534" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Jacobian Determinant"}] :question/id #uuid "eec994b4-82ac-568c-b493-dc9a21102c2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-11156" :question/problem "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/eec66e1a6fdb16b157ec4ba8ea32b8e2328c26910594a7d21bb7346669797627.png)\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/01df906c78b9ed5b4d6f1e29b4aeeb3be607b7570db357c4dce5949702432011.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/62dec67282c5bc2707377cb976412a48ca3c41ffeed6aa74ef0d5b1ef8d6e380.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = 2v\\cos{u}$ and $y(u,v) = v\\sin{u}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡u-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\  & =-2v({sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u) \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/72902f1a278bc1316ff58c54940b4d5e65fa04aa8e1711c8e0a1726f57cd3641.png) the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\\right|_{(0,-1)} = -2(-1) = 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128795 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82be73-0720-5267-8698-427641b0e66e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Area Scale Factor of a Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "32f92fce-b4b9-5e7a-9fe0-255b15edf312" :question/math-academy-id "e-12417" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $\\Delta$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-1,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta{A}.$ Find an approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/4c41597b6c647ba16bff7b6675d1bbe9d3c66b8cbb8e9a13bbb6651da6cd284e.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}\\big(\\mathbf{T}(\\Delta)\\big) \\approx \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\cdot \\underbrace{\\textrm{Area}(\\Delta)}_{\\large \\delta{A}}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{v}^{3} & 3u{v}^{2} \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={v}^{3}\\cdot 2v-3u{v}^{2}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{v}^{4}-6{u}^{2}{v}^{2} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,2)} & =(2{v}^{4}-6{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\,{|}_{u=-1\\,v=2} \\\\  & =2(2)^{4}-6(-1)^{2}(2)^{2} \\\\  & =32-24 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|8|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =8\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128796 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"}] :question/id #uuid "6102567f-7035-5fa2-b12e-80a0470de4a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12418" :question/problem "Sketch the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/0e620d5ca71a48c87a0d5942ffa8378964f6172bff7396c290024a5299531204.png)\nis (locally) orientation-reversing." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}  <    0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/e947cb962ec1c0405292be18c838205b7be95ab083e07925d797b87e9c5b8654.png)\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{v} & u{e}^{v} \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{v}\\cdot (-2v)-u{e}^{v}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-2{e}^{v}({u}^{2}+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{v}({u}^{2}+v) & <0 \\\\ {u}^{2}+v & >0 \\\\ v & >-{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-u^2\n$$\n\ndefines a downward-opening parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/2b6128c5a1737e3ad1fd82f91f5ccd320421ad3472ae8152440034ecea10d364.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128941 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d56cec4a-6121-5988-aaea-478fb6048a6d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian Determinant of a Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "28562339-0fe0-5d56-8767-2ec0834525d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6810" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/ce4692599c5c9fe69ed9247d65dee5d982e1b7a2cf33b9070b799c94c321bad4.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/59265be9c026880542032006b0c4aec9f1c8eeb2cd448423d38c17f75e1393cd.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=(u+v)^3$ and $y=(u-v)^2.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3(u+v)^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =3(u+v)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2(u-v) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3(u+v)^{2} & 3(u+v)^{2} \\\\ 2(u-v) & -2(u-v)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(u+v)^{2}\\cdot (-2(u-v))-3(u+v)^{2}\\cdot 2(u-v) \\\\  & =-12(u+v)^{2}(u-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a70becb4-79f8-5997-94b7-5f28df55f8eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1999/Source/Images/q-160341-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126435} {:answer/id #uuid "d81549fa-8d14-5411-b716-bd6fe31e9c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1999/Source/Images/q-160341-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126436} {:answer/id #uuid "f061e33e-5529-5193-9672-0bc670bf7886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1999/Source/Images/q-160341-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126437} {:answer/id #uuid "3a178425-276d-58c2-ba03-99a817cf8677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1999/Source/Images/q-160341-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126438} {:answer/id #uuid "63be98e5-4a9b-518e-8d73-bfb0258b1e47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1999/Source/Images/q-160341-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d81549fa-8d14-5411-b716-bd6fe31e9c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1999/Source/Images/q-160341-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126436} :answer-field/id #uuid "0748c909-b892-5f58-ab20-be0bd6bcc6f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5ab6f10-630f-5f61-bd52-33e1fa95452d" :question/math-academy-id "q-160341" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-2{v}^{2} \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-2{v}^{2} \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -4v \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-1)-(-4v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2u+4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2u+4v & <0 \\\\ 4v & <2u \\\\ v & <\\frac{1}{2}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{2}u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/09f7f36ecc2197cc95e1878fa22ac22367978809a5241f09886a7eb2fab071f3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d56cec4a-6121-5988-aaea-478fb6048a6d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian Determinant of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4943fd7-0a18-5215-90ac-f6c4a5dea350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321382} {:answer/id #uuid "67016959-0a81-5c30-ba53-215b69609898" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321383} {:answer/id #uuid "970c567f-8304-5a40-bfe1-1b5f2a6a55f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321384} {:answer/id #uuid "00320c3a-7ce6-5428-b38a-a15312303290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u+2v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321385} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3b8ff2-95b1-5296-9834-965f62e1744e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3u-v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67016959-0a81-5c30-ba53-215b69609898" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321383} :answer-field/id #uuid "894c9b06-246e-54fb-88ee-61a694e6c6d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51ad204a-d196-50d6-898f-5c163645b62a" :question/math-academy-id "q-160381" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3u+2v \\\\ u+3v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3u+2v$ and $y(u,v)=u+3v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =9-2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab643519-92a4-5ccf-b826-fb2a68b36534" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Jacobian Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b2d0948-335e-5a83-935d-32686d857beb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321389} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c75532-4145-5779-a1c6-d0ef33956809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321390} {:answer/id #uuid "668e24b8-b1dc-5d8e-9a52-c9a99eb204d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321391} {:answer/id #uuid "867e1f04-35b3-5248-b39a-e2753a1bdf26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321392} {:answer/id #uuid "6570a49d-14c0-5934-ac06-4602f930254b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6570a49d-14c0-5934-ac06-4602f930254b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321393} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdd4d8e8-ebdd-5a44-b693-2dc6b1d9eca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33d8b05e-b106-5110-93ab-68951418f20b" :question/math-academy-id "q-160383" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}uv \\\\ \\frac{v}{u}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=uv$ and $y=\\frac{v}{u}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ -\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v\\cdot \\frac{1}{u}\\text{ }-u\\cdot (-\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2v}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,3)}=\\frac{2(3)}{-2}=-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab643519-92a4-5ccf-b826-fb2a68b36534" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Jacobian Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a23ef7cd-4733-51c7-8408-9ff974ae78b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126465} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ad17fa-bfe1-5d51-972f-33672ba83a2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126466} {:answer/id #uuid "b52fee2e-327c-583c-a75b-01457e5dab4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126467} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd8624c-3292-5856-8ca5-533d07b41c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126468} {:answer/id #uuid "211f50fa-07bd-538b-8015-614dc5e473c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "211f50fa-07bd-538b-8015-614dc5e473c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126469} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebec0485-6d94-5e35-99b8-b348a79e849f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa6e908a-36ba-5f5c-b35a-6753290d1954" :question/math-academy-id "q-160394" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ {e}^{u-v}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u+v}$ and $y(u,v)={e}^{u-v}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & ={e}^{u-v} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-{e}^{u-v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ {e}^{u-v} & -{e}^{u-v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot (-{e}^{u-v})-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot {e}^{u-v} \\\\  & =-{e}^{2u}-{e}^{2u} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(0,-5)}=-2{e}^{2(0)}=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab643519-92a4-5ccf-b826-fb2a68b36534" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Jacobian Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30b656a6-f88c-533c-a7db-249a4ed42aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321396} {:answer/id #uuid "53750778-69b0-57e4-ba96-a2c5c8813ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321397} {:answer/id #uuid "f602365b-0275-532d-9709-78e67dfaf473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321398} {:answer/id #uuid "1902cb62-efd5-5f06-b22b-250fc85e637e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321399} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7e9509-e423-5dbf-a6dc-1407cc326ec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53750778-69b0-57e4-ba96-a2c5c8813ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321397} :answer-field/id #uuid "8146d7b5-c520-56bd-a3c2-89e72fc89945" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ccfd3db3-a315-55cd-ab25-4bedc4ab7dc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-160402" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u-v}$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv).$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u-v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u-v} & -{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u-v}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{v})-(-{e}^{u-v})\\cdot \\frac{1}{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u-v}}{v}+\\frac{{e}^{u-v}}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,1)}=\\frac{{e}^{(1)-(1)}}{(1)}+\\frac{{e}^{(1)-(1)}}{(1)}=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d56cec4a-6121-5988-aaea-478fb6048a6d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian Determinant of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8afb8e13-3520-55c3-beaa-e9c12bfba3c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{u+v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321403} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8438c1-645d-5940-b341-fb316dd9a053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{u+v}}{vu}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321404} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c55101-c4e5-5ba3-aee1-092beb434ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321405} {:answer/id #uuid "4872c6ad-e082-510c-8de8-1cb972ac3e2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321406} {:answer/id #uuid "68676031-c9c7-5563-93a0-91d73cdf8c06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{u+v}(u-v)}{vu}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68676031-c9c7-5563-93a0-91d73cdf8c06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{u+v}(u-v)}{vu}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321407} :answer-field/id #uuid "9875af00-227c-51a7-861f-6e7f885e8dc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56e2ae8b-95c6-5996-94d4-12dc0977c236" :question/math-academy-id "q-160403" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T([\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv)\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u+v}$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv).$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{v})-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{u}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u+v}}{v}-\\frac{{e}^{u+v}}{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u+v}(u-v)}{vu}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d56cec4a-6121-5988-aaea-478fb6048a6d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian Determinant of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aa8b965-5712-5d6d-8ba8-9b4f24f4a5b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321410} {:answer/id #uuid "9892b6d7-5959-559e-ae4a-e19665bd2aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321411} {:answer/id #uuid "0ac3b561-4133-5596-b7c5-1484af713362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321412} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb62695-f3af-59af-9dd5-8573d65673a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321413} {:answer/id #uuid "5e05c483-0ed9-5fbb-96e6-fcc7d98ef241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ac3b561-4133-5596-b7c5-1484af713362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321412} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bf4b50c-e6a4-5f92-9f9a-6fe81a30f46b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7156c5ac-a4a8-5169-962b-59a363668107" :question/math-academy-id "q-160405" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T([\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ 4u+6v\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)$ and $y=4u+6v.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\,\\text{ }4\\, & \\,\\text{ }6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\cdot 6-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v))\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab643519-92a4-5ccf-b826-fb2a68b36534" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Jacobian Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "347ef92f-72e3-527d-bd48-8391bbfa39e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321417} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ffe5be-9691-58ac-98b7-45f8df2813b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321418} {:answer/id #uuid "45b512fd-b757-5114-9976-a37d819156b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321419} {:answer/id #uuid "eb265da9-bf32-5326-beef-0f99d10e661a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321420} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f7ff23-f245-5524-bf18-8d2fcbeb3136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb265da9-bf32-5326-beef-0f99d10e661a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321420} :answer-field/id #uuid "fda924d6-7d31-5a62-9046-028948374d00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "762a86f1-8402-51e3-be2b-f70d1f3cadfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-160428" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}}{2} \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\frac{{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}}{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}u & -v \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =u\\cdot u-(-v)\\cdot v \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,-2)}=(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab643519-92a4-5ccf-b826-fb2a68b36534" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Jacobian Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37673c99-bc74-5ef6-85a2-e0e90b4986b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321424} {:answer/id #uuid "20f8a967-717b-59da-aa84-f27f15ecf454" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321425} {:answer/id #uuid "e796a470-b6e9-5fc2-956b-9fb66fdcfa22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321426} {:answer/id #uuid "7c5efd10-13c2-53a0-927b-60d1283b9da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321427} {:answer/id #uuid "17ec3825-fa01-5933-898a-f1b0a7b7082c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37673c99-bc74-5ef6-85a2-e0e90b4986b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321424} :answer-field/id #uuid "c64f920c-a042-57c7-a992-1102d1fd8656" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5213d980-a132-5e4e-960f-5a8c11058e91" :question/math-academy-id "q-160440" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi /6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡v-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi /6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(\\frac{\\pi }{6},0)}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82be73-0720-5267-8698-427641b0e66e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Area Scale Factor of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f51e42d0-86fc-55a9-a8d9-ed171e4f1106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321431} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a816e2-c515-596e-8328-fe3491451466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321432} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f626c7-2bf0-5ecc-93cb-3fd24b7dd656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321433} {:answer/id #uuid "ecfb9684-e70c-53ca-87d1-ce22a81bb026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321434} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e0e9b2-1ba5-501d-a03d-a02b6abd70ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecfb9684-e70c-53ca-87d1-ce22a81bb026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321434} :answer-field/id #uuid "65f2f7c7-8a7d-52df-9828-dafeed9e1231" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db66022b-312f-5359-a47c-3da72da0979b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161831" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-1,1)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}v{e}^{u} \\\\ u{e}^{v}\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v{e}^{u} & {e}^{u} \\\\ {e}^{v} & u{e}^{v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v{e}^{u}\\cdot u{e}^{v}-{e}^{v}\\cdot {e}^{u} \\\\  & =uv{e}^{u+v}-{e}^{u+v} \\\\  & =(uv-1){e}^{u+v} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,1)} & =(uv-1){e}^{u+v}\\,{|}_{u=-1\\,v=1} \\\\  & =(-1\\cdot 1-1){e}^{-1+1} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-2|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =2\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6357bf8b-1482-5211-9a7c-29b32ad00d57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/d5889bd5e8d537757de25a7b1a868ff8a903da14c9088c5f8de5a70d8b078552.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321438} {:answer/id #uuid "0325c7c6-0b05-54d6-b88a-a3dea017a901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/cb5b22ff46569b231d122c2ed11bd412907f36271a033d86769435adf9fe48c7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321439} {:answer/id #uuid "284819b2-e283-5689-a01d-e1d9883573b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321440} {:answer/id #uuid "a1323d4b-5d6c-5d05-b7ce-5f224d94e07d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/3033f0ae73e3f484fc5d3d65e388062feba1824fb3da9971d560cf2f518f49bb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321441} {:answer/id #uuid "d088b455-8f2a-557d-9731-a266c019af1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/b0171dfd42295e39cd45186e8edba3a5105fbd1bd02a1621223091de75815f61.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0325c7c6-0b05-54d6-b88a-a3dea017a901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/cb5b22ff46569b231d122c2ed11bd412907f36271a033d86769435adf9fe48c7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321439} :answer-field/id #uuid "57de394d-fefc-5046-84e6-3484649715e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2f0da55-1926-59a4-96c8-e68855af7a42" :question/math-academy-id "q-161837" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+v \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+v \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2} & 1 \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot u-v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={u}^{3}-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}-v & <0 \\\\ v & >{u}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv={u}^{3}\n$$\n\ndefines a cubic function, and the coordinates of, say, $(-1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/cb5b22ff46569b231d122c2ed11bd412907f36271a033d86769435adf9fe48c7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "215b13e5-218c-5539-b548-62e6f8434957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/8382e54c1f91ff0e07910c1250c046413814748d9e7ef8968984b7223b43afcc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321445} {:answer/id #uuid "b4efcc9c-8cb8-51af-83cb-0d25a1531941" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/14015cadab7d5dc8a9c33d0c1a57a44a79506bd01ba9b1cfe8ec1d280eb3fbb6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321446} {:answer/id #uuid "20a43105-8be0-55b1-8ad4-a9e3709f225b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/7f214af0b5d71689ec7f21fff043e1779f1d3244c13fdbaf7b8df7f408d66298.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321447} {:answer/id #uuid "e38bf910-3583-5750-8c79-881b34471120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/3033f0ae73e3f484fc5d3d65e388062feba1824fb3da9971d560cf2f518f49bb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321448} {:answer/id #uuid "11ea278b-acf9-5666-97bc-401c8a4d5625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "215b13e5-218c-5539-b548-62e6f8434957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Devel