) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/8b35fbafaf8d121bceef7a0763253df075fe4fe85eb3c992f95c59d05f986c67.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1122d3f-ff29-5cb8-8015-e8edef091955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/1368ee41963c1abcc320d2cd35449c08599582f37c7d68f750e137fcdcd2e57b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321459} {:answer/id #uuid "3e4e0673-9394-5e85-b2b7-6fc5f5374903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/613e83ce5ad6154905ec9001ece078309e396561e251e85455ff261bb4cd61a0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321460} {:answer/id #uuid "74763255-fd2c-5df7-8856-04ad1f4cb2ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/7fc8ce1e1eaa3eb4e4acbbc167bdc4a479591bea21d2acdeaf799bd99d6b1150.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321461} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5b70e4-6ba4-5d95-bc10-903be175a9b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321462} {:answer/id #uuid "0503e98c-14fd-5283-aaf2-e95c7136af13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/9ecab1b0f048e83b57d04a60c1cf20ee5ed0e61257577cf42c1a091976c04473.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e4e0673-9394-5e85-b2b7-6fc5f5374903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/613e83ce5ad6154905ec9001ece078309e396561e251e85455ff261bb4cd61a0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321460} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6de591a-b4f0-5e48-bec9-2bb805c1fc06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79f8fa2f-9fe2-5bd3-a5f8-69303258e3fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-161855" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{uv} \\\\ {u}^{2}+v\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{uv} \\\\ {u}^{2}+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v{e}^{uv} & u{e}^{uv} \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v{e}^{uv}\\cdot (1)-(2u)\\cdot u{e}^{uv} \\\\  & ={e}^{uv}(v-2{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{uv}(v-2{u}^{2}) & >0 \\\\ v-2{u}^{2} & >0 \\\\ v & >2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=2{u}^{2}\n$$\n\ndefines a parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(0,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/613e83ce5ad6154905ec9001ece078309e396561e251e85455ff261bb4cd61a0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82be73-0720-5267-8698-427641b0e66e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Area Scale Factor of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2c3375e-3569-5ed2-9137-30b6f4aebfdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321466} {:answer/id #uuid "b3298e03-11de-5d79-9dea-868e02292082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321467} {:answer/id #uuid "11915dde-f642-54f2-b35c-ef31d6adb1f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321468} {:answer/id #uuid "a96ef44c-e861-527e-b55a-be46d8452794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321469} {:answer/id #uuid "06773dc3-9683-5dbb-b25c-0487820119a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a96ef44c-e861-527e-b55a-be46d8452794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321469} :answer-field/id #uuid "62590c22-ba9a-5dfa-ac4f-6bdb5615a80f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321465}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b443c39-074e-5076-be2c-75de4128c444" :question/math-academy-id "q-161874" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(4,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{u}+{v}^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} & 2v \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot u-v\\cdot 2v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{u}-2{v}^{2} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(4,2)} & ={\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{u}-2{v}^{2}\\,|}_{u=4\\,v=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4}-2(2)^{2} \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-7|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =7\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82be73-0720-5267-8698-427641b0e66e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Area Scale Factor of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da346313-1e8e-553a-8240-a7edb8a09607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321473} {:answer/id #uuid "9d540d39-4f0e-5bea-8019-d200f006fcea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321474} {:answer/id #uuid "43d1167b-6740-5d26-96f8-337500b6bf09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321475} {:answer/id #uuid "fac05473-6650-5687-b38e-e1e35d24b7e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321476} {:answer/id #uuid "bc9026b1-40fe-5f7e-a69f-dda32476c884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fac05473-6650-5687-b38e-e1e35d24b7e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321476} :answer-field/id #uuid "0134db05-accd-5d29-beed-0459695e61ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc1b537d-1bb4-5840-be74-781a73ccdf95" :question/math-academy-id "q-161876" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-2,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ {u}^{3}+v\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ 3{u}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot 1-3{u}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{u+v} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}(1-3{u}^{2}) \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,2)} & ={e}^{u+v}(1-3{u}^{2})\\,{|}_{u=-2\\,v=2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2+2}(1-3(-2)^{2}) \\\\  & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-11|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =11\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82be73-0720-5267-8698-427641b0e66e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Area Scale Factor of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1165d820-8c9a-530b-bea4-1f500654617c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321480} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0961c2-2482-5e4c-8f0e-91315448fade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321481} {:answer/id #uuid "e535e0eb-ceff-574c-adc1-770f7a40c3d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321482} {:answer/id #uuid "778aab89-4930-5665-8b02-d6081c7c0f8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321483} {:answer/id #uuid "558bb363-d711-573b-bd51-8d27ba594b03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b0961c2-2482-5e4c-8f0e-91315448fade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321481} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcd4b40e-8263-5a62-bab9-83a9515e604b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8da66ba0-baf0-55db-8a40-0cff8a5bd366" :question/math-academy-id "q-161885" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(2,4)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2u}{v} & -\\frac{{u}^{2}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2u}{v}\\cdot u-v\\cdot (-\\frac{{u}^{2}}{{v}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{u}^{2}}{v}+\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,4)} & =(\\frac{3{u}^{2}}{v})\\,{|}_{u=2\\,v=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(2)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|3|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =3\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82be73-0720-5267-8698-427641b0e66e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Area Scale Factor of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1b8aca2-a24d-5291-9ff4-ef0374976569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321487} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0801fc-f3a1-5698-aa28-54287f267ef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321488} {:answer/id #uuid "4a2b70be-617d-54a6-a3d7-df4bd68930bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321489} {:answer/id #uuid "ac987553-2ca2-5650-845a-8f70c5f6df8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321490} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea96360-423a-5ed4-9d02-4dbce91a0790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac987553-2ca2-5650-845a-8f70c5f6df8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321490} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4a27b2d-ad2d-51d5-8728-36f4074f18dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf8e0377-ce4d-5d4d-975a-f98752347ead" :question/math-academy-id "q-161887" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(1,4)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{uv} \\\\ u{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{v}}{2\\sqrt{u}} & \\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{2\\sqrt{v}} \\\\ {v}^{2} & 2uv\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{v}}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 2uv-{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{2\\sqrt{v}} \\\\  & =v\\sqrt{uv}-\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,4)} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv})\\,|}_{u=1\\,v=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)\\sqrt{(1)(4)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|4|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =4\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d56cec4a-6121-5988-aaea-478fb6048a6d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian Determinant of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "135c6ea6-f46c-5927-8b06-75426bddb92e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127203} {:answer/id #uuid "5be889c6-c1d6-5a1a-a5be-c2b7aa374869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127204} {:answer/id #uuid "6b7d7e7a-5832-5c17-bda6-7b283bed1de5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127205} {:answer/id #uuid "72e4385f-6172-58f7-9434-1f032e953971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127206} {:answer/id #uuid "74c6c789-4d05-5ec9-a0e4-4debebbe96d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b7d7e7a-5832-5c17-bda6-7b283bed1de5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127205} :answer-field/id #uuid "7196d11f-207e-5091-ab68-aca7b90010dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127202}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0beb6dad-ec13-5ce0-a209-c7b3cfc3efa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-38078" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ 3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$ and $y(u,v)=3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot 3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-(-2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =6u({cos}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v) \\\\  & =6u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d56cec4a-6121-5988-aaea-478fb6048a6d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian Determinant of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ab097c8-fd2e-5294-b764-ae11be7adedc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321494} {:answer/id #uuid "e558ee73-5bd8-52fb-926c-447f7c9cb4ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v){sin}^{2}⁡(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321495} {:answer/id #uuid "42331668-5e01-55a8-94d6-a6ee432d6290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321496} {:answer/id #uuid "fa40c07e-265a-511c-b9cd-1cd4c1a23cca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321497} {:answer/id #uuid "2952e5ed-028e-5f82-9691-328a98c64b70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa40c07e-265a-511c-b9cd-1cd4c1a23cca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321497} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1afaefd-4350-5818-8b7b-7a9c63c65e34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cecd2a95-7cec-5199-a231-42148dcd4eda" :question/math-academy-id "q-81252" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T([\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v).$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)\\, & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
